  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Showery precipitation tonight with lowering snow levels to \n  bring localized wintry travel, mainly passes.\n\n- A moderate system for Friday night will likely cause moderate \n  travel impacts in the region, mainly I-90 southward.\n\n- Another round of light valley snow potential in northwest \n  Montana Sunday night/Monday morning. \n\nWidespread precipitation continues across the Northern Rockies \nthrough early evening as the remnants of an atmospheric river sags\nthrough the region. Snow levels have risen to around 6000 feet \nwith limited impacts at pass levels overall. Precipitation turns \nshowery tonight as snow levels drop closer to 4500-5000 feet by \nFriday morning. Brief heavy showers may bring occasional snow \ncovered roads above 5000 feet but winter driving impacts should \nremain localized. \n\nThe next weather system of interest comes together Friday \nafternoon and lasts into early Saturday as a disturbance drops \nthrough in northwest flow. Widespread precipitation is expected \nonce again, mainly from I-90 southward. Pass level impacts are \nexpected, including the higher valleys across southwest Montana, \nthus winter weather advisories have been added. There is still \nconsiderable uncertainty with snow levels for west-central Montana\nincluding Missoula early Saturday morning, there is a 25% chance \nof moderate snow impacts even to valley floors. Much will depend \non the intensity of the precipitation and how quickly cold air \narrives, for now the forecast is patterned toward precipitation \ndiminishing as snow levels reach valley floors (and only light \naccumulations) but certainly a period to watch. Overall, plan for \nslick roads over passes and along I-90 in southwest Montana Friday\nnight. Scattered showers continue on Saturday but wintry impacts \nlook to be minimal once the main activity of the early morning \ndiminishes. \n\nAnother wave of moisture arrives on Sunday afternoon and lasts \ninto Tuesday. Snow levels across northwest Montana may initially \nbe at valley floors through Monday morning with light valley snow \naccumulation potential leading to slick roads. Overall \nprecipitation amounts are light but passes should once again see \nwintry impacts to travel.",
